                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose the transfer gap`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `Six months in, the East Africa P&L is at <strong>38% of plan</strong> — a <strong>US$5.6M</strong> gap. You shipped Atlântico's European go-to-market playbook untouched: the direct fleet reaches <strong>11%</strong> of outlets while <em>68% of grocery volume flows through informal dukas</em> it can't serve, chilled SKUs spoil at <strong>22%</strong> versus 3% in Iberia, and only 3 of 24 hires have category experience. Separate what genuinely <strong>transfers</strong> — brand, recipes, quality, finance discipline — from what must be <strong>locally adapted</strong> — route-to-market, cold chain, pack sizes, talent. Then name the single biggest source of the gap.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Redesign the operating model`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Reconfigure go-to-market and cold chain under the budget. <strong>Channel mix:</strong> route volume through informal-trade aggregators to reach the dukas, or stay direct modern-trade and cap near 11%. <strong>Cold chain:</strong> invest in chilled depots (CapEx, margin retained) or reformulate toward ambient SKUs (low spoilage, lower margin) — keeping the full chilled portfolio with no spend destroys <em>~US$6.2M of product a year</em>, more than the whole revenue gap. <strong>Pack architecture:</strong> add affordable KSh sachet price points for the duka shelf. Fund the redesign or it won't move reach.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Build local management capacity`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Five expats at US$220K each is <strong>US$1.1M a year</strong> — and builds zero local capability. Design the people model that replaces expat dependence: set the <strong>localisation curve</strong> (majority-local leadership by Month 24), choose <strong>build-vs-buy</strong> — a graduate academy and structured development (PGGE/PAGAP-style) that grows durable local GMs, versus poaching experienced managers at a premium — and pair every expat with a <em>named local successor</em>. Shift budget off expat salaries toward capability building. When the board reviews this in 24 months, <strong>who runs the business?</strong>`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Choose the partner and commit`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Two partners are pushing for a decision and a South-African competitor is circling your JV partner. The <strong>distributor</strong> gets you reach by Q2 but takes <em>14 points of margin forever</em> while you learn nothing about the customer. The <strong>50/50 JV</strong> needs US$7M and deeper control — but a JV without governance (board seats, decision rights, an exit clause) fails on partner alignment, and if you hesitate the competitor signs your partner. A <strong>hybrid</strong> buys speed now and a JV option later. Choose the mode that fits the model you actually built — and re-run the 3-year financials with <em>your</em> channel, cold-chain and people decisions in them.`,

