                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_adapt:         `<strong>That's the right diagnosis.</strong> Brand, recipes, quality systems and finance discipline travel; route-to-market, the cold-chain assumption, pack sizes and the talent model do not. You're no longer optimising a Lisbon model in Nairobi — now decompose the gap and rank the causes.`,
                    coach_r1_execution:     `<strong>You've added two trucks and three salespeople — and you'd reach 14% of outlets instead of 11%.</strong> 68% of the volume is in dukas your direct model can't serve. You're optimising a model built for Lisbon. What part of the operating model itself is wrong for Nairobi?`,
                    coach_r1_market:        `<strong>The market is 55 million people growing at 5%.</strong> 'Kenya isn't ready' is the comfortable answer that lets you avoid redesigning your model. The opportunity is real; your operating model is the thing that isn't ready.`,
                    coach_r1_people:        `<strong>Flying in more expats treats the symptom and burns the budget.</strong> Five expats at US$220K is US$1.1M a year and zero local capability. The team looks inexperienced because the model gives them no way to win. Fix the model, then build the people.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You read it as a transfer gap AND named route-to-market as the biggest single source — the direct fleet at 11% against 68% of volume in dukas. That's the lever that unlocks the others; everything you redesign next should serve that channel.`,
                    coach_r1_rootcap:       `<strong>The capability gap is real, but it's a symptom, not the gap.</strong> Even category-experienced hires can't sell through a fleet that reaches 11% of outlets. The route-to-market mismatch is the larger number — fix the channel and the people have something to execute.`,
                    coach_r1_lowpriority:   `<strong>Almost all your effort is on execution, not the model.</strong> That's how you end up reaching 14% instead of 11% and calling it progress. Put real weight on re-engineering route-to-market, cold chain and pack sizes — that's where the US$5.6M is.`,
                    coach_r2_modern:        `<strong>You're still mostly direct modern-trade.</strong> That's the channel that caps you near 11%. The 68% of volume sits in dukas you only reach through aggregators and wholesalers — route real volume through the informal channel or the reach won't move.`,
                    coach_r2_coldkeep:      `<strong>You kept all 40% of chilled SKUs and budgeted nothing for cold chain.</strong> At a 22% spoilage rate that's ~US$6.2M of product destroyed a year — more than your entire revenue gap. Do you build the cold chain, or do you change the portfolio? Doing neither is the most expensive choice on the board.`,
                    coach_r2_coldhybrid:    `<strong>Matching the product to the infrastructure — that's the move.</strong> Chilled depots where the cold chain actually exists, ambient SKUs everywhere else. You've cut the spoilage that was destroying more than the revenue gap, without over-building cold infrastructure that doesn't pay back.`,
                    coach_r2_packnone:      `<strong>European pack sizes don't sell at the duka shelf.</strong> A shopper buying for the day can't afford a family pack. Without affordable KSh sachet price points, the reach you buy through aggregators doesn't convert into volume.`,
                    coach_r2_underfunded:   `<strong>You're redesigning on a shoestring.</strong> US$1M doesn't onboard aggregators, fix cold chain and re-architect packs at once. Underfunding the redesign is how a good strategy still misses — the reach barely moves.`,
                    coach_r3_academy:       `<strong>Building local GMs is the strategy, not an HR afterthought.</strong> A graduate academy and structured development is slow and it works — in 24 months you have people who can run this business, and the expat burn falls away. This is the Rafael / PGGE-PAGAP play.`,
                    coach_r3_blend:         `<strong>A lean expat core over an academy pipeline is pragmatic.</strong> You keep enough experience to execute now while building the local bench that replaces it. Just make sure the transfer is structured, not hopeful — pair every expat with a named successor.`,
                    coach_r3_poach:         `<strong>Poaching buys capability fast — at a premium and with low loyalty.</strong> It plugs the gap but builds no pipeline of your own; the managers you bought can be bought again. Pair it with an academy or you'll be poaching forever.`,
                    coach_r3_expat:         `<strong>Five expats at US$220K each is US$1.1M a year — and you've built zero local capability.</strong> When the programme is reviewed in 24 months, who runs this business? Show me the localisation curve, because right now there isn't one.`,
                    coach_r3_slowcurve:     `<strong>Your localisation curve is too slow.</strong> A board member will challenge it as exactly that. If local leadership is still a minority at Month 24, the expat burn never ends and there's no succession — the capability KPI is the one the board is watching.`,
                    coach_r3_notransfer:    `<strong>No knowledge-transfer mechanism means the capability leaves when the expats do.</strong> Shadowing 'when there's time' transfers nothing. Name the successors and put a timeline on the handover, or you're renting capability, not building it.`,
                    coach_r4_distributor:   `<strong>The distributor gets you reach by Q2 — and takes 14 points of margin forever while you learn nothing about the customer.</strong> The competitor is circling your JV partner. Speed today, or capability and control tomorrow? Defend the trade-off — and don't pretend a distributor builds the local business.`,
                    coach_r4_jv:            `<strong>The JV gives you control, shared cold-chain assets and a real capability-transfer channel — for US$7M.</strong> That's the mode that fits the model you built. Now govern it: board seats, decision rights, brand discipline, an exit clause. The choice isn't the hard part; the alignment is.`,
                    coach_r4_jvnogov:       `<strong>You committed US$7M to a 50/50 JV with no governance.</strong> What happens when your partner wants volume and cash while you want brand discipline and capability transfer? Without board seats, decision rights and an exit clause, partner alignment — not partner choice — is what sinks this.`,
                    coach_r4_jvlost:        `<strong>You waited, and the South-African competitor signed your JV partner.</strong> The best partner and a full season of trade are gone. In an entry race, hesitation past the deadline is itself a decision — and you made it.`,
                    coach_r4_hybrid:        `<strong>Distributor now, JV option later is a defensible hedge.</strong> You buy speed without giving up the path to control — provided the option is contractual and you actually exercise it once capability is built. Don't let 'later' become 'never'.`,
                    coach_r4_nogov:         `<strong>Even an asset-light partnership needs governance.</strong> An exclusive distributor with no service levels or exit terms controls your route to market and your customer relationship. Define the decision rights now, while you still have leverage.`,
                    coach_r4_incoherent:    `<strong>Your 3-year P&L still assumes European pack sizes and modern-trade margins.</strong> None of your Round 2 redesign or Round 3 people plan shows up in the numbers. Re-run it with the model you actually chose — coherence between strategy and the financial case is the whole pitch.`,

