                    insight_won:            `Model adapted and won: you stopped exporting Lisbon and re-engineered for Nairobi — informal-channel reach, a cold chain matched to the infrastructure, affordable sachets, local GMs replacing the expat cadre, and a partner that fits the model and is governed for alignment. The US$5.6M gap closed on a model the competitor will struggle to copy.`,
                    insight_export:         `Copy-paste export: the European operating model went to Kenya largely untouched. Whether through unfixed cold chain or expat dependence, you optimised a Lisbon playbook in a market it was never built for — and the board's pause trigger is exactly this outcome.`,
                    insight_partial:        `Partial turnaround: some decisions adapted to local conditions and others didn't, so the model still half-belongs to Lisbon. The pieces don't yet reinforce each other into one coherent operating model.`,
                    insight_cold:           `Cold-chain unresolved: chilled spoilage is still destroying product before it sells — the single most expensive assumption that didn't transfer. Match the product to the infrastructure or fund the cold chain; doing neither costs more than the whole revenue gap.`,
                    insight_expat:          `Local capacity stayed thin: too much reliance on flown-in expatriates and too little succession built. In 24 months there is still no clear answer to 'who runs this business' — building local GMs was the strategy, and it was under-played.`,
                    insight_reach:          `Reach stayed low: most volume still routes through a direct fleet that can't serve the dukas where 68% of the market shops. The route-to-market mismatch — the biggest single source of the gap — was never fully fixed.`,
                    insight_partner:        `Partner alignment failed: either the JV ran without governance, or hesitation lost the partner to the competitor. The entry-mode decision was right or wrong less on the choice than on alignment and timing — and that is where it broke.`,
                    insight_moat:           `The deeper win: by adapting the model rather than exporting it — local channel, local cold-chain design, local GMs — you built something the next entrant can't simply copy from a European playbook. That is the durable advantage.`,

                    hint_cold:              `Chilled spoilage is still high — it's destroying more product than your entire revenue gap. Build distributed cold chain where it exists and reformulate to ambient everywhere else.`,
                    hint_expat:             `You're still leaning on expatriates with no real localisation curve. Shift budget to a local academy and structured succession — the board will ask who runs this in 24 months.`,
                    hint_reach:             `Reach is still near the modern-trade ceiling. Route real volume through informal-trade aggregators and add affordable sachet price points, or you'll never serve the 68% in the dukas.`,
                    hint_partner:           `Your partner relationship is exposed — ungoverned or slipping to the competitor. Lock in board seats, decision rights and an exit clause, and commit before the partner is gone.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — reach climbing, spoilage controlled and local capacity building. Choose the partner that fits this model and govern it for alignment to hold the gain.`,

